{
  "_comment": "Antigravity (agy) permission baseline. Merged by setup.sh into ~/.gemini/antigravity-cli/settings.json via claude/scripts/agy-apply-permissions.py (that file also holds trustedWorkspaces and per-machine grants, so it is never symlinked). Rules are word-by-word prefixes. Precedence: deny > ask > allow. Security model: the terminal sandbox is the boundary (toolPermission=proceed-in-sandbox, enableTerminalSandbox=true); it blocks ~/.ssh and .env files by default and isolates the filesystem and network. No local code runner or text writer carries an unsandboxed(...) rule; only gh subcommands, git push, and the review/shipping gates run unsandboxed (git fetch/pull prompt, because repository config can name programs for git to run). Reads outside the workspace are served inside the sandbox through allowNonWorkspaceAccess and read_file(~/...) rules, which the merge script expands to the machine's home; never add read_file(~/...) rules for $HOME symlinks that resolve into ~/dev/ (such as ~/.bash_aliases or ~/.gitconfig), because bwrap resolves realpath on read_file rules and mounts them read-only on top of the read-write ~/dev/ mount. read_file denies cover credential files exactly, because the file tool matches paths, not shell text. Withdrawing a rule from the permissions object does not withdraw it from a machine that already installed it, because apply is additive; the retired array lists allow grants the baseline has revoked, and retired_ask lists baseline prompt gates the baseline has revoked. apply deletes any exact match in retired from allow and in retired_ask from ask, and check fails until they are gone; operator-added ask rules for retired allow grants and all deny rules are never touched, because removing them would weaken the operator's own hardening. There are deliberately no command regexes: shell syntax cannot be classified airtight (see the reverted #411), so command denies are plain prefixes that act as speed bumps inside the sandbox, not as a boundary.",
